  "notes": "Four versicle-and-response pairs said bowed at the foot of the altar after the absolutions, the last of them the first Dóminus vobíscum of the Mass. The pairs are drawn from the psalms and follow the shape of the Office's preces. VOBÍSCUM at w033 is one written word carrying two: the preposition cum is written after the pronoun rather than before it, as it always is with the personal pronouns (mecum, tecum, nobíscum). It is tagged as the pronoun it is built on — vos, ablative plural, the case cum governs — and the enclitic is explained in the note. Collatinus dictionaries the fused form as a lemma of its own and tags it singular, which the pipeline sets aside by a recorded ruling — hence the medium confidence and the disputed review, as at ordinarium.pax-domini w005. CONVÉRSUS at w003 is a perfect passive participle used with an active sense (turning, not being turned), a reflexive reading the Latin psalter keeps from its Greek; the tag records the form, the note the sense.",
